Chapter 3 – IP Addresses
Part 1 – The Language of Computer Networks
________________________________________
Learning Objectives
By the end of this part, you will be able to:
•	Define an IP address.
•	Explain why every device needs an IP address.
•	Distinguish between IP addresses and MAC addresses.
•	Understand IPv4 and IPv6 at a high level.
•	Explain why computers use numbers instead of names.
•	Recognize public and private IP addresses.
Estimated Study Time: 2–3 hours
Prerequisites:
•	Chapter 1 – What Is a Computer Network?
•	Chapter 2 – How the Internet Really Works
________________________________________
Chapter Review
In the previous chapter, you learned that when you typed:
https://www.google.com
DNS translated the domain name into something like:
142.250.xxx.xxx
That number is called an IP address.
But what exactly is an IP address?
Why does every device need one?
That's what we'll answer in this chapter.
________________________________________
Imagine Delivering a Letter
Suppose you want to send a birthday card to your friend.
Would writing only:
John
be enough?
Of course not.
There are millions of people named John.
You need an address like:
John Smith
123 Rizal Street
Lipa City
Batangas
Philippines
Now the postal service knows exactly where to deliver the letter.
Computer networks work the same way.
________________________________________
What Is an IP Address?
Definition
An Internet Protocol (IP) Address is a unique numerical address assigned to a device on a network.
Its purpose is to identify a device so that information can be sent to the correct destination.
Think of it as a home's mailing address—but for computers.
________________________________________
Why Do We Need IP Addresses?
Imagine millions of computers connected together.
Without addresses:
•	Messages would have nowhere to go.
•	Routers couldn't make forwarding decisions.
•	Devices couldn't communicate.
Every packet sent across a network contains:
•	Source IP address
•	Destination IP address
Without these, communication would fail.
________________________________________
Real-World Example
Suppose your laptop has this IP address:
192.168.1.25
Your home router might have:
192.168.1.1
When your laptop wants to access the Internet, it sends packets toward the router, which then forwards them toward their destination.
________________________________________
What Does an IP Address Look Like?
The most common version today is IPv4.
Example:
192.168.1.25
It consists of four numbers separated by periods.
Each number ranges from:
0–255
Example:
10.0.0.1

172.16.5.22

192.168.100.14

8.8.8.8
________________________________________
Why Numbers?
Humans prefer names.
Computers prefer numbers.
Imagine asking someone to locate:
"The blue house beside the mango tree."
That description can change.
Now imagine giving GPS coordinates.
Much more precise.
IP addresses give computers precise destinations.
________________________________________
IP Address vs Domain Name
These two are often confused.
Domain Name	IP Address
google.com	142.250.xxx.xxx
youtube.com	142.xxx.xxx.xxx
github.com	140.82.xxx.xxx
Humans remember domain names.
Computers communicate using IP addresses.
DNS connects the two.
________________________________________
IP Address vs MAC Address
Another common source of confusion.
IP Address	MAC Address
Logical address	Physical hardware address
Can change	Usually stays with the network interface
Used between networks	Used within the local network
Assigned by network configuration	Assigned by the manufacturer
Think of it this way:
House Address → IP Address
Person's Fingerprint → MAC Address
We'll study MAC addresses in detail in a later chapter.
________________________________________
IPv4
IPv4 stands for:
Internet Protocol Version 4.
It has been the backbone of the Internet for decades.
Example:
203.177.42.15
Advantages:
•	Simple
•	Widely supported
•	Easy to understand
Limitation:
There are only about 4.3 billion possible IPv4 addresses.
When IPv4 was designed, engineers never imagined billions of smartphones, smart TVs, IoT devices, and cloud servers.
Eventually, IPv4 addresses became scarce.
________________________________________
IPv6
IPv6 is the newer version of the Internet Protocol.
Example:
2001:0db8:85a3:0000:0000:8a2e:0370:7334
At first glance, it looks intimidating.
Don't worry—we'll study IPv6 in a dedicated chapter.
For now, remember:
•	IPv4 uses four decimal numbers.
•	IPv6 uses hexadecimal numbers separated by colons.
•	IPv6 provides an enormous number of unique addresses.
________________________________________
Public vs Private IP Addresses
Not every IP address can be used directly on the Internet.
There are two main categories.
Public IP Address
A public IP address is reachable from the Internet.
It is assigned by your Internet Service Provider (ISP).
Example:
203.177.42.15
If someone on the Internet communicates with your home network, they usually communicate through your public IP address.
________________________________________
Private IP Address
Private IP addresses are used inside local networks.
Common ranges include:
10.0.0.0 – 10.255.255.255

172.16.0.0 – 172.31.255.255

192.168.0.0 – 192.168.255.255
You will commonly see addresses like:
192.168.1.2

192.168.1.10

192.168.1.100
These addresses are not directly reachable from the public Internet.
________________________________________
Real-Life Example
Suppose your home has:
•	Laptop
•	Smartphone
•	Tablet
•	Smart TV
•	Printer
Each device receives its own private IP address.
Your entire home network then shares a single public IP address provided by your ISP.
In the next part of this chapter, you'll learn how this is possible using Network Address Translation (NAT).
________________________________________
