Networking Fundamentals: From Zero to Professional Network Engineer
Chapter 3 – IP Addresses
Part 2 – NAT, DHCP, and Static vs. Dynamic IP Addresses
________________________________________
Learning Objectives
By the end of this part, you will be able to:
•	Explain why one public IP address can serve an entire home or office.
•	Understand what Network Address Translation (NAT) is and why it exists.
•	Differentiate between static and dynamic IP addresses.
•	Understand the purpose of DHCP.
•	Explain how a device receives an IP address when it joins a network.
•	Recognize how these technologies support modern networking.
Estimated Study Time: 3–4 hours
Prerequisites:
•	Chapter 1 – What Is a Computer Network?
•	Chapter 2 – How the Internet Really Works
•	Chapter 3 – Part 1
________________________________________
Review
In Part 1, you learned:
•	Every device needs an IP address.
•	There are public and private IP addresses.
•	IPv4 addresses are limited.
•	IPv6 was introduced to help solve address exhaustion.
But one important question remains:
If your home has 15 devices, why does your ISP usually give you only one public IP address?
The answer is Network Address Translation (NAT).
________________________________________
The Problem NAT Solves
Imagine a family of five living in one house.
The house has:
•	One street address
•	Five people living inside
When someone sends a letter, the postal service only needs the house address.
Once the letter arrives, the family knows who it belongs to.
A home network works in a similar way.
________________________________________
What Is NAT?
Definition
Network Address Translation (NAT) is a process performed by a router that translates private IP addresses into a public IP address (and back again).
This allows many devices on a private network to share a single public IP address.
________________________________________
Why Was NAT Created?
IPv4 provides about 4.3 billion addresses.
That sounded like a lot in the 1980s.
Today we have:
•	Smartphones
•	Laptops
•	Tablets
•	Smart TVs
•	Security cameras
•	Smart speakers
•	IoT devices
•	Cloud servers
Without NAT, IPv4 addresses would have been exhausted much sooner.
________________________________________
How NAT Works
Imagine your home network:
                 Internet
                     │
          Public IP: 203.177.42.15
                     │
               Home Router
       ┌────────┼────────┬────────┐
       │        │        │
192.168.1.10 192.168.1.20 192.168.1.30
 Laptop      Phone       Smart TV
Each device has its own private IP address.
To the Internet, however, they all appear to come from 203.177.42.15.
The router keeps track of which device started each connection so it can send the responses back to the correct device.
________________________________________
Step-by-Step Example
Suppose your laptop wants to visit example.com.
Step 1
Laptop sends:
Source:
192.168.1.10

Destination:
93.184.216.34
________________________________________
Step 2
Router performs NAT.
It changes the source address to:
203.177.42.15
Now the packet appears to come from your home's public IP address.
________________________________________
Step 3
The website replies to:
203.177.42.15
________________________________________
Step 4
The router checks its NAT table and determines:
"This response belongs to Laptop (192.168.1.10)."
It forwards the packet to the laptop.
The website never needs to know the laptop's private IP address.
________________________________________
NAT Table (Simplified)
Private Device	Public Address
192.168.1.10	203.177.42.15
192.168.1.20	203.177.42.15
192.168.1.30	203.177.42.15
The router uses additional information, such as port numbers, to keep each connection separate. You'll learn about ports in a later chapter.
________________________________________
Advantages of NAT
•	Conserves IPv4 addresses.
•	Allows many devices to share one public IP.
•	Hides private IP addresses from the Internet.
•	Simplifies home and small business networking.
________________________________________
Limitations of NAT
•	Adds some complexity to networking.
•	Certain applications may require extra configuration (such as port forwarding).
•	Does not replace a firewall or other security controls.
________________________________________
Static vs. Dynamic IP Addresses
Not every IP address stays the same.
There are two common assignment methods.
Static IP Address
A static IP address remains the same until it is manually changed.
Examples of devices that often use static IPs:
•	Servers
•	Printers
•	Network switches
•	Routers
•	Security cameras
Advantages
•	Predictable
•	Easier to locate on the network
•	Useful for hosting services
Disadvantages
•	Manual configuration
•	More administrative effort
________________________________________
Dynamic IP Address
A dynamic IP address is automatically assigned and may change over time.
Most home devices use dynamic addresses.
Examples:
•	Smartphones
•	Laptops
•	Tablets
These devices often receive their addresses automatically whenever they connect to the network.
________________________________________
What Is DHCP?
Definition
Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP) is a network service that automatically assigns IP addresses and other network settings to devices.
Without DHCP, you would have to manually configure every new device that joined the network.
________________________________________
Why DHCP Exists
Imagine a company with 2,000 computers.
Assigning IP addresses manually would be slow and error-prone.
Instead, a DHCP server automatically provides:
•	IP address
•	Subnet mask
•	Default gateway
•	DNS server
This makes network management much easier.
________________________________________
The DHCP Process (Simplified)
When a new device joins the network:
1. Device: "Does anyone have an IP address for me?"

↓

2. DHCP Server: "Yes, here's one."

↓

3. Device: "I'd like to use it."

↓

4. DHCP Server: "Approved."
This exchange happens in just a few moments.
________________________________________
Real-World Example
You arrive at a coffee shop and connect to its Wi-Fi.
Within seconds:
•	Your phone receives a private IP address.
•	It learns the default gateway.
•	It learns which DNS server to use.
You don't have to enter these settings manually because DHCP does the work.
________________________________________
Practical Applications
NAT and DHCP are used in:
•	Homes
•	Offices
•	Universities
•	Hotels
•	Airports
•	Coffee shops
•	Data centers
Almost every modern network uses DHCP, and most home networks also rely on NAT.
________________________________________
Case Study
Problem
A user reports that they cannot connect to the Internet.
Investigation
The administrator checks the device and finds it has an address beginning with:
169.254.x.x
This indicates the device did not receive a valid IP address from DHCP.
Solution
The administrator restarts the DHCP service and renews the device's IP lease.
The device receives a proper address and connectivity is restored.
________________________________________
