Networking Fundamentals: From Zero to Professional Network Engineer
Chapter 3 – IP Addresses
Part 3 – Subnet Masks, Network IDs, and Host IDs
________________________________________
Learning Objectives
By the end of this part, you will be able to:
•	Explain what a subnet mask is.
•	Understand why subnetting exists.
•	Distinguish between the Network ID and Host ID.
•	Determine whether two devices are on the same network.
•	Understand CIDR notation at a beginner level.
•	Recognize why subnetting is important for performance and security.
Estimated Study Time: 3–4 hours
Prerequisites:
•	Chapter 1 – What Is a Computer Network?
•	Chapter 2 – How the Internet Really Works
•	Chapter 3 – Parts 1 & 2
________________________________________
Chapter Review
So far you've learned:
•	Every device needs an IP address.
•	Devices can have public or private IP addresses.
•	NAT allows many devices to share one public IP.
•	DHCP automatically assigns IP addresses.
Now another question arises:
How does your computer know whether another device is nearby on the same network or somewhere else on the Internet?
The answer lies in the subnet mask.
________________________________________
The Problem
Imagine a company with 5,000 computers.
If every computer tried talking directly to every other computer, the network would become slow, inefficient, and difficult to manage.
Instead, engineers divide large networks into smaller networks, called subnets.
Think of it like organizing a city into neighborhoods rather than treating the entire city as one giant street.
________________________________________
What Is a Subnet?
Definition
A subnet (short for subnetwork) is a smaller network created by dividing a larger network.
Instead of placing every device into one huge network, devices are grouped into logical sections.
Examples:
•	HR Department
•	Finance Department
•	Engineering Department
•	Guest Wi-Fi
•	Security Cameras
Each can have its own subnet.
________________________________________
Why Create Subnets?
Subnetting improves:
•	Performance
•	Security
•	Organization
•	Troubleshooting
•	Scalability
Instead of broadcasting information to thousands of devices, communication stays within the appropriate subnet whenever possible.
________________________________________
Analogy
Imagine a university.
Without departments:
Everyone works in one enormous office.
Finding the correct professor would be chaotic.
Instead, the university has:
•	Engineering Building
•	Medical Building
•	Business Building
•	Library
Each building is easier to manage.
Subnets work the same way.
________________________________________
What Is a Subnet Mask?
Definition
A subnet mask is a number that tells a computer:
Which part of an IP address identifies the network, and which part identifies the individual device (host).
Example:
IP Address
192.168.1.25

Subnet Mask
255.255.255.0
The subnet mask acts like a divider.
________________________________________
Network ID vs. Host ID
Every IPv4 address has two parts:
Network Portion | Host Portion
The subnet mask determines where the split occurs.
Example:
IP Address

192.168.1.25

Subnet Mask

255.255.255.0
Result:
Network ID
192.168.1

Host ID
25
This means:
The device belongs to network:
192.168.1.0
and its unique identifier within that network is:
25
________________________________________
Real-World Example
Consider three computers:
Device	IP Address
PC A	192.168.1.10
PC B	192.168.1.25
PC C	192.168.1.200
Subnet Mask:
255.255.255.0
All three belong to:
192.168.1.0
Therefore, they are on the same subnet.
________________________________________
Now consider another computer:
192.168.2.10
Notice the third number changed.
This computer belongs to a different network.
Communication between these networks usually requires a router.
________________________________________
Visual Illustration
Subnet A

192.168.1.0/24

 ┌─────────────┐
 │ Laptop      │
 │192.168.1.10 │
 └─────────────┘

 ┌─────────────┐
 │ Phone       │
 │192.168.1.20 │
 └─────────────┘

 ┌─────────────┐
 │ Printer     │
 │192.168.1.50 │
 └─────────────┘


        │
     Router
        │


Subnet B

192.168.2.0/24

 ┌─────────────┐
 │ Server      │
 │192.168.2.15 │
 └─────────────┘
________________________________________
CIDR Notation
You may see subnet masks written differently.
Instead of:
255.255.255.0
You might see:
/24
This is called CIDR notation.
Examples:
CIDR	Subnet Mask
/24	255.255.255.0
/16	255.255.0.0
/8	255.0.0.0
For now, simply remember:
/24

=

255.255.255.0
You'll learn how CIDR works mathematically in the subnetting chapter.
________________________________________
Why Does Your Computer Need the Subnet Mask?
Suppose your computer wants to send data to:
192.168.1.50
It compares:
Its own IP:
192.168.1.25
Target IP:
192.168.1.50
Subnet Mask:
255.255.255.0
Result:
Both devices belong to the same network.
Therefore:
Your computer sends the data directly on the local network.
________________________________________
Now imagine the destination is:
192.168.2.50
Your computer recognizes that this address belongs to a different network.
Instead of sending directly, it forwards the packet to the default gateway (router).
The router then forwards the packet to the correct network.
________________________________________
Why Is This Important?
Without subnet masks:
•	Devices wouldn't know where local networks end.
•	Routers couldn't efficiently forward traffic.
•	Large networks would become slow and difficult to manage.
Subnetting is one of the most important concepts in networking.
________________________________________
Security Benefits
Subnetting isn't only about organization.
It also improves security.
Example:
Instead of placing everyone on one network:
Employees
Guests
Servers
Security Cameras
You separate them.
Result:
If a guest device becomes infected with malware, it is much harder for the attack to spread to sensitive systems.
This practice is often combined with VLANs and firewall rules, which you'll learn later in the course.
________________________________________
Practical Applications
Subnetting is used in:
•	Enterprise networks
•	Universities
•	Hospitals
•	Government agencies
•	Cloud environments
•	Data centers
Every professional network uses subnetting.
________________________________________
Case Study
Problem
A company placed:
•	Employee laptops
•	Guest Wi-Fi
•	Servers
all on the same subnet.
An infected guest laptop scanned every server on the network.
Solution
The network administrator created separate subnets and applied firewall rules between them.
Result
Guest devices could access the Internet but could not directly communicate with internal servers, greatly reducing the risk of lateral movement by attackers.
